            <ArticleTitle>Low-friction arthroplasty for healed septic and tuberculous arthritis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>144-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Forty hips, which had previously been the site of tuberculous or pyogenic arthritis and which had later developed a degenerative arthritis, were treated by low-friction arthroplasty some forty years after the original infection. The results suggest that, when healing of the primary infection has been followed by a long period of quiescence with acceptable function before the onset of degenerative change, the arthroplasty can be confidently expected to result in greatly improved function and that this improvement is long-lasting.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
